      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:724",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:724",
        "x": "5395",
        "y": "130",
        "width": "1100",
        "height": "177",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "STAGE 2 — WHICH HIP PROBLEM IS IT?\nWhere it hurts does most of the work at the hip: groin or front → the joint or the groin muscles; side → the gluteal tendons; buttock → hamstring, sacroiliac joint or deep buttock. Age then separates osteoarthritis from young-adult hip pain."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:916",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:916",
        "x": "6575",
        "y": "130",
        "width": "560",
        "height": "251",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "WHY THE LABEL MATTERS MORE AT THE HIP\nGluteal tendinopathy needs specific position advice (avoid compression); hip-related pain needs activity modification around deep bending; groin problems in athletes need targeted strengthening. Hip osteoarthritis still runs on the shared core programme. Software should map label → programme module + positions to avoid, not pick exercises per patient."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:780",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:780",
        "x": "100",
        "y": "2321",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "488",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HD1 · WORKING DIAGNOSIS: Hip osteoarthritis\nWHAT IT IS: “wear and repair” changes in the hip joint; the commonest cause of groin pain in older adults. No X-ray is needed to start care; the guideline keeps X-ray for when the picture is unclear.\nNEXT STEP: exercise therapy is the main treatment (strongest recommendation, grade A, JOSPT 2025): individualised strengthening in all directions plus mobility work, typically 1–5 sessions a week for 5–16 weeks; education on pacing and protecting the joint; a walking stick in the OPPOSITE hand if it keeps them active; practise their goal tasks (rising from the floor, getting into a vehicle); group programmes such as GLA:D where available (NICE NG226).\nAVOID: bracing early in care; passive treatments on their own; weight-loss advice for an underweight patient.\nSTAGE 3 ADD-ON: hip osteoarthritis module."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:768",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:768",
        "x": "220",
        "y": "2075",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "176",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Fits: weight-bearing pain, stiffness under 1 hour, limited or painful internal rotation"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:772",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:772",
        "x": "670",
        "y": "2075",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "153",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Rotation normal and the pain is on the side → side-of-hip check (HT3)"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:776",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:776",
        "x": "1000",
        "y": "2075",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "176",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Younger than typical, with a risk factor (steroids, alcohol, sickle cell) → back to H5"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:764",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:764",
        "x": "490",
        "y": "1441",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "564",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HT1 · Hip osteoarthritis check (JOSPT hip osteoarthritis guideline, Cibulka 2017; revised 2025)\nTypical: age 50+; moderate groin, front or side hip pain with weight-bearing; morning stiffness under 1 hour; trouble with socks, shoes, cross-legged sitting and squatting.\nKey sign — internal rotation (hip and knee bent 90°, swing the foot outward so the hip turns inward): less than 24°, OR internal rotation and bending both 15° less than the other side, and/or pain on passive internal rotation.\nExtra confidence — Sutlive cluster (count 5): 1) squatting aggravates; 2) active hip bending causes side-hip pain; 3) the scour test with adduction causes side-hip or groin pain; 4) active hip extension (leg backward) is painful; 5) passive internal rotation 25° or less. 4 of 5 → LR+ 24.3, probability of osteoarthritis about 91% (Sutlive 2008; small study, not yet validated).\nACR clinical criteria: sensitivity 86%, specificity 75% (Altman 1991).\nWHO: physio-level (rotation); anyone trained (history)"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:752",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:752",
        "x": "610",
        "y": "1241",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "50 or older (from 45 if the pattern is typical)"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:812",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:812",
        "x": "1330",
        "y": "2199",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "725",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HD2 · WORKING DIAGNOSIS: Hip-related pain — FAI syndrome, hip dysplasia or labral tear (Zurich consensus, Reiman 2020)\nWHAT IT IS: in young and middle-aged active adults: FAI (femoroacetabular impingement — extra bone on the ball or socket pinches during deep bending), dysplasia (a shallow socket), or a tear of the labrum (the cartilage rim of the socket).\nCONFIRM: groin pain with long sitting, deep squats, getting in and out of vehicles, or pivoting; clicking or catching; positive FADIR. FADIR catches 96–99% of cases but is also positive in many people without the problem — a NEGATIVE test helps rule it out; a positive test doesn’t confirm it (Reiman 2015). The subtype cannot be known without imaging, and the exercise approach is shared.\nNEXT STEP: physio-led care: activity modification + exercise (guideline grade B), movement-pattern training and education (grade C) (JOSPT, Enseki 2023). Hip and trunk strength from isometric to heavy; practise squats, stairs and vehicle transfers.\nAVOID EARLY: repeated deep bending with rotation (deep squats, long cross-legged sitting) while irritable — this collides with floor sitting and squatting toilets, so plan around it.\nREFER (X-ray, orthopaedic opinion) IF: no progress by about 12 weeks.\nSTAGE 3 ADD-ON: young-hip module."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:788",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:788",
        "x": "1450",
        "y": "1999",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "A) FADIR reproduces their groin pain"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:816",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:816",
        "x": "1900",
        "y": "2199",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "419",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HD3 · WORKING DIAGNOSIS: Adductor-related groin pain\nWHAT IT IS: an overloaded inner-thigh (adductor) tendon where it attaches near the pubic bone; very common in kicking and cutting sports (football, kabaddi, cricket).\nCONFIRM (Doha): tender adductor tendon + pain on resisted squeeze.\nNEXT STEP: adductor isometrics (squeeze holds), then progressive strengthening such as side-lying and Copenhagen-style adduction; hip and trunk strength; graded return to sprinting, kicking and change of direction.\nAVOID: full kicking and sprinting while the squeeze test is painful.\nSTAGE 3 ADD-ON: groin module."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:792",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:792",
        "x": "2020",
        "y": "1999",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "B) Squeeze or adductor tendon"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:820",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:820",
        "x": "2470",
        "y": "2199",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "350",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HD4 · WORKING DIAGNOSIS: Iliopsoas-related groin pain\nWHAT IT IS: an overloaded deep hip-flexor muscle (iliopsoas) at the front of the groin, from kicking, sprinting or repeated hip bending.\nCONFIRM (Doha): tender iliopsoas + pain on resisted hip bending and/or stretch.\nNEXT STEP: hip-flexor isometrics, then progressive strengthening; trunk and hip strength.\nAVOID: aggressive hip-flexor stretching while irritable.\nSTAGE 3 ADD-ON: groin module."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:796",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:796",
        "x": "2590",
        "y": "1999",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "C) Resisted hip bending or stretch"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:824",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:824",
        "x": "3040",
        "y": "2199",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "396",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HD5 · WORKING DIAGNOSIS: Inguinal-related groin pain\nWHAT IT IS: pain at the inguinal canal (the passage just above the groin crease), with no hernia.\nCONFIRM (Doha): pain and tenderness at the inguinal canal, no palpable hernia, worse with resisted sit-ups, coughing or straining.\nNEXT STEP: graded trunk (abdominal) and hip strengthening.\nAVOID: heavy straining while painful.\nREFER IF: a bulge appears (surgical referral; sometimes an ultrasound is needed to tell the difference).\nSTAGE 3 ADD-ON: groin module."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:800",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:800",
        "x": "3160",
        "y": "1999",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "D) Sit-up, cough or inguinal canal — no lump"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:804",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:804",
        "x": "3610",
        "y": "1999",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "153",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "D) A lump that bulges on coughing → hernia, back to H9"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:828",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:828",
        "x": "3940",
        "y": "2176",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "373",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HD6 · WORKING DIAGNOSIS: Pubic-related groin pain\nWHAT IT IS: irritation of the pubic joint at the front of the pelvis; sometimes after pregnancy, more often in kicking and running sports.\nCONFIRM (Doha): local tenderness of the pubic symphysis and the bone right beside it.\nNEXT STEP: graded loading as for adductor-related pain (HD3).\nREFER IF: hopping is painful — a bone stress injury needs imaging (H4).\nSTAGE 3 ADD-ON: groin module."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:808",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:808",
        "x": "4060",
        "y": "1999",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "107",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "E) Pubic bone tender"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:784",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:784",
        "x": "2635",
        "y": "1441",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "488",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HT2 · Groin test set for active people (Doha agreement, Weir 2015)\nDo each and note which one reproduces their FAMILIAR pain — more than one can be positive:\nA) FADIR — hip bent to 90°, moved across the body (adduction) and turned inward (internal rotation).\nB) Adductor squeeze — squeeze a fist or ball between the knees; press the inner-thigh tendon near the pubic bone.\nC) Hip-flexor tests — resist lifting the knee while seated; stretch the front of the hip lying at the edge of a bed (Thomas test position); press just below the groin crease.\nD) Abdominal tests — resisted sit-up and a cough; press over the inguinal canal (just above the groin crease) and feel for a lump.\nE) Pubic bone — press directly on the joint at the front midline of the pelvis (pubic symphysis).\nWHO: physio-level"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:756",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:756",
        "x": "2755",
        "y": "1241",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "16–49 and active (sport, heavy work)"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:760",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:760",
        "x": "4510",
        "y": "1241",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Under 16 → back to H3 (child with a limp)"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:748",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:748",
        "x": "2460",
        "y": "966",
        "width": "480",
        "height": "205",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HQ2 · How old, and how active?\nWHY: hip osteoarthritis is the main cause of groin pain in older adults; in younger active adults, the shape of the hip joint and the groin muscles are more likely causes."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:732",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:732",
        "x": "2560",
        "y": "766",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Groin or front of the hip (or the C-sign)"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:848",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:848",
        "x": "4840",
        "y": "1754",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "541",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HD7 · WORKING DIAGNOSIS: Gluteal tendinopathy (side-of-hip pain; often called “trochanteric bursitis”)\nWHAT IT IS: the tendons of the buttock muscles that attach to the bony point on the side of the hip are overloaded and compressed.\nNEXT STEP: education on load + progressive abductor exercise. In the LEAP trial this beat a steroid injection and “wait and see” at 8 weeks, with better overall improvement than injection at 1 year (Mellor 2018).\n• Avoid compressing the tendon: no crossing the legs, hanging on one hip, lying on the painful side without a pillow, or very low seats; a pillow between the knees when side-lying.\n• Isometric side holds for pain relief → bridges, step-ups, single-leg work.\nAVOID: stretches across the side of the hip (they compress the tendon); steroid injection as the first plan; complete rest.\nSTAGE 3 ADD-ON: side-of-hip module."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:836",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:836",
        "x": "4960",
        "y": "1531",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "153",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Tender AND pain on single-leg stance (or another loading test)"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:840",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:840",
        "x": "5410",
        "y": "1531",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "176",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Not tender on the bony point → check the hip joint (HT1) and the back (H10)"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:844",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:844",
        "x": "5740",
        "y": "1531",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "153",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Burning or numbness rather than tenderness → HD8"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:832",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:832",
        "x": "5230",
        "y": "966",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "495",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HT3 · Side-of-hip check (gluteal tendinopathy — Grimaldi 2017, 65 patients compared with MRI)\n1. Press the bony point on the side of the hip (greater trochanter). NOT tender → gluteal tendinopathy is unlikely (palpation sensitivity 80%).\n2. Single-leg stance 30 s: side-on to a wall, one fingertip on the wall, stand on the painful leg for up to 30 seconds. Side-hip pain within 30 s → very likely (specificity 100%, LR+ about 12; small study).\n3. Physio add-ons: FADER (hip bent, moved across the body and turned OUTWARD) and passive adduction lying on the side — specificity about 87% each.\nHISTORY: can’t lie on that side at night; stairs, standing on one leg, crossing the legs and low seats hurt; more common in women from middle age.\nWHO: anyone trained (steps 1–2); physio-level (step 3)"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:736",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:736",
        "x": "5350",
        "y": "766",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Side of the hip, over the bony point"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:888",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:888",
        "x": "6070",
        "y": "2160",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "373",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HD9 · WORKING DIAGNOSIS: Hamstring strain injury\nWHAT IT IS: a tear in the muscle at the back of the thigh, usually from sprinting or over-stretching.\nNEXT STEP — staged loading: early pain-free isometrics and walking → progressive strengthening including slow lengthening (eccentric) work, with Nordic-type exercises later → graded running up to sprinting. Return when strength and range are close to the other side (Martin 2022).\nAVOID: aggressive stretching early; sprinting before strength and range have recovered.\nSTAGE 3 ADD-ON: hamstring module."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:880",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:880",
        "x": "6190",
        "y": "1960",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Tender muscle + pain on resisted testing"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:884",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:884",
        "x": "6640",
        "y": "1960",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "130",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Gap or big bruise at the sitting bone → back to H11"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:876",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:876",
        "x": "6295",
        "y": "1517",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "373",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HT4 · Hamstring strain check (JOSPT hamstring guideline, Martin 2022)\n• Find the sorest point and how far the tenderness spreads.\n• Lying face down, resist knee bending at 90° and at 15° — pain and weakness?\n• Active knee extension range: lying, hip held at 90°, straighten the knee; compare sides (used to track recovery).\nPOSITIVE IF: tender muscle + pain and weakness on resisted testing.\nDANGER SIGN: a gap or big bruise at the sitting bone after a slip into the splits → H11.\nWHO: physio-level"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:860",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:860",
        "x": "6415",
        "y": "1271",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "176",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Sudden pain at the back of the thigh while sprinting, kicking or over-stretching"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:892",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:892",
        "x": "6970",
        "y": "1517",
        "width": "520",
        "height": "419",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HD10 · WORKING DIAGNOSIS: Proximal hamstring tendinopathy\nWHAT IT IS: the hamstring tendon is irritated where it attaches to the sitting bone.\nCONFIRM: pressing the sitting bone (side-lying, hip bent) reproduces the pain; lying with hip and knee bent, straightening the knee hurts at the sitting bone.\nNEXT STEP: tendon loading without compression in deep hip bending — isometric bridge holds, then progress from shallow to deeper hip bending; a cushion on hard seats (consensus).\nAVOID EARLY: deep hamstring stretches and long sitting on hard surfaces.\nSTAGE 3 ADD-ON: hamstring module."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:864",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:864",
        "x": "7090",
        "y": "1271",
        "width": "280",
        "height": "176",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "Deep pain right at the sitting bone; worse on hard seats, uphill or bending forward"
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:908",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:908",
        "x": "7540",
        "y": "2121",
        "width": "440",
        "height": "212",
        "name": "ROUNDED_RECTANGLE"
      },
      "text": "HX1 · SACROILIAC JOINT PAIN → LOW-BACK PATHWAY\nManage with lumbopelvic and hip strengthening and graded activity; a pelvic belt short-term for pregnancy-related pain. Don’t label it as hip pain."
    },
    {
      "source_id": "cNyP7TFyrcoIYVPUoQlMgw:5:900",
      "type": "shape-with-text",
      "attributes": {
        "id": "5:900",
        "x": "7620",
        "y": "1944",
        "width": "280",
